/* Educational content for Learn only. The investment tools use their existing data. */
window.ASSET_GUIDE_LESSONS = {
eq: {
intro:[
"A share is a claim on a company’s residual value: employees, suppliers, lenders and other senior claimants are paid before common shareholders. Owning a share gives you exposure to the business, but not the right to demand a dividend or redeem the share at a fixed price. Stock exchanges match buyers and sellers; most everyday trades transfer existing shares rather than finance the company.",
"Separate three questions: Is the business durable? Is its balance sheet resilient? What expectations are already built into the price? A fine company can be a poor investment at the wrong price, and a low valuation can be justified by permanent deterioration."
],
types:[
["Common shares","Ordinary shares usually carry voting rights and any dividends the board declares. Some companies have multiple classes with unequal votes or transfer rights, so economic ownership does not necessarily confer proportional control.","Shareholders benefit from growing profits and cash flows per share, but new share issuance, stock-based pay or acquisitions paid in stock can dilute ownership. In insolvency, common equity is last in line and may be wiped out.","Read the share-class terms and check voting power, diluted share count and the sources of free cash flow."],
["Depositary receipts (ADRs and GDRs)","A depositary bank issues receipts that represent a specified number or fraction of underlying foreign shares. ADRs are associated with the US market; GDRs may trade in other international markets. A programme may be sponsored by the company or arranged without its participation.","A receipt quoted in your home currency does not remove foreign-exchange exposure. Depositary fees, dividend withholding, voting arrangements, home-market restrictions and programme termination can affect the outcome.","Check the receipt-to-share ratio, programme documents, home-market listing, fees, liquidity and how corporate actions are handled."],
["Growth, value and dividend stocks","These are investing styles, not separate legal securities. Growth investors emphasise the future expansion of earnings or cash flow; value investors look for a gap between price and estimated worth; dividend investors favour regular distributions. One company can fit several styles or move between them.","Growth shares can fall sharply when expectations or valuations reset. Cheap shares can be value traps if cash flows keep deteriorating. A high dividend yield may reflect a falling share price and an expected cut rather than a bargain.","Test the growth needed to justify the price, compare normalised earnings with peers and assess whether cash flow and debt headroom support dividends."],
["Large-, mid-, small- and micro-cap stocks","Market capitalisation is share price times shares outstanding. Size cutoffs vary by market and index provider; they do not describe business quality. A benchmark may use free-float-adjusted market cap, excluding shares not readily available for trading.","Smaller businesses may have narrower product lines, fewer funding options and wider trading spreads. Large firms may be more diversified, but size does not prevent disruption, leverage or concentration in a few customers or business lines.","Check free float, trading volume, financing needs, customer concentration and whether a position can be sold at a reasonable cost."],
["Initial public offerings (IPOs) and later issuance","An IPO is a first public offering. Primary shares raise capital for the issuer; secondary shares let existing holders sell. The offering price is not necessarily the price available once exchange trading begins. Follow-on offerings occur after listing.","Short operating histories, concentrated voting control and a limited initial float can complicate valuation. Expiry of a lock-up can increase tradable supply; it does not guarantee a price fall. A direct listing and a merger with a listed acquisition vehicle are different routes to public trading.","Read the prospectus for use of proceeds, dilution, related-party transactions, lock-ups, control rights and audited financial statements."]
],
returns:[
"Total return combines price change with distributions. If a €50 share ends the year at €54 and pays €2 in dividends, its simple gross return is (€54 − €50 + €2) ÷ €50 = 12%, before fees and tax. In practice, reinvestment timing matters. On the ex-dividend date, price typically adjusts for the payout, all else equal.",
"Over time, business results and the valuation applied to them both matter. If earnings per share rise 10% but the price-to-earnings multiple falls from 20 to 16, the share price falls 12% before dividends. Buybacks can boost each remaining share’s ownership percentage, but repurchasing expensive shares or borrowing heavily to do so can destroy value."
],
terms:[["EPS and diluted EPS","Profit attributable to common shareholders per weighted-average share; diluted EPS also reflects qualifying potential shares."],["P/E, P/B and PEG","Price relative to earnings or book value; PEG relates P/E to expected growth. Negative earnings, accounting choices and uncertain forecasts can make these poor shortcuts."],["Enterprise value and EV/EBITDA","Enterprise value adjusts equity value for debt, cash and other claims where relevant. EBITDA excludes capital spending and is not free cash flow."],["Free cash flow and FCF yield","Operating cash flow less capital expenditure is a common starting point. Divide equity-level cash flow by equity value, or use a consistent enterprise-level measure."],["ROE and leverage","Return on equity relates profit to shareholders’ equity. Borrowing can lift ROE while increasing risk; review debt-to-equity, interest coverage and the current ratio."],["Dividend yield and payout ratio","Annual dividends divided by price, and dividends divided by earnings, respectively. Compare cash payout with recurring cash generation."],["DCF and margin of safety","Discount future cash flows to today under several assumptions. Margin of safety is the gap between price and estimated value, not a guarantee. The Graham number is a historical screen, not a substitute for valuation."]],
checks:["Read the annual report, financial statements, footnotes and auditor’s opinion. Reconcile profits with operating cash flow, and distinguish recurring earnings from one-off gains.","Track margins, debt maturities, share count and capital allocation over several years. Compare business economics and valuation with genuinely comparable firms.","Build a base, upside and downside case. Write down what would disprove your thesis, and check exposure across sectors, geography and currencies."],
mistakes:["Treating a low multiple, high dividend yield or fast revenue growth as proof of value.","Using a single-year earnings figure for a cyclical company, or overlooking dilution and off-balance-sheet commitments.","Mistaking a foreign share’s listing currency for its economic currency exposure."],
reading:[["Investor.gov: Stocks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks"],["SEC investor bulletin: American depositary receipts","https://www.sec.gov/investor/alerts/adr-bulletin.pdf"],["SEC investor bulletin: Investing in an IPO","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-17"]]
},
debt:{
intro:[
"A bond is a contractual claim, not ownership. The investor lends to an issuer and receives cash flows under specified terms. A conventional bond has a face value, payment schedule and maturity, but some bonds amortise principal, change their coupon, allow early repayment or have no fixed maturity.",
"Read fixed income in two layers. First ask what the contract promises; then ask how likely the issuer is to pay and what the promise is worth today. If required yields rise, the present value of existing fixed cash flows falls. Yields can rise because benchmark interest rates move, credit spreads widen, or both."
],
types:[
["Government and agency bonds","National treasuries issue sovereign debt. Short bills and longer notes and bonds fund government borrowing. Agency or supranational debt has a different obligor; some issues have explicit guarantees and others do not.","Sovereign risk varies by country and currency. Foreign-currency debt can be harder for an issuer to service in stress, while domestic-currency payments can lose real value through inflation. A high-quality long bond can still lose substantial market value when rates rise.","Identify the legal issuer, currency, guarantee, duration, inflation exposure and whether the bond is governed by domestic or foreign law."],
["Local authority and municipal bonds","Regional or local bodies issue debt for general budgets or specific projects. A general-obligation bond generally relies on a public issuer’s taxing or fiscal capacity; a revenue bond relies on designated income such as utility fees or tolls, subject to local terms.","Revenue bonds can be exposed to project delays, lower usage and operating costs. Some municipal bonds have local tax advantages, but treatment depends on the issue, jurisdiction and investor.","Check the pledged revenue, budget and pension obligations, debt-service coverage, call provisions and yield after tax."],
["Corporate bonds: investment grade and high yield","Companies issue debt to fund operations, investment or refinancing. Investment grade is generally BBB−/Baa3 or higher on major agency scales. High yield is below that range; an unrated bond is not automatically equivalent to either category.","A high spread may compensate for default, poor recovery, illiquidity or all three. Secured, senior unsecured and subordinated debt can recover very different amounts from the same issuer. A downgrade can cause a price loss even when payments continue.","Review interest coverage, free cash flow, leverage, secured assets, covenants, near-term maturities and comparable spreads. Ratings do not replace this work."],
["Inflation-linked bonds","Many link principal to a specified price index; coupon payments then change because the coupon rate applies to adjusted principal. Designs vary by issuer, including the inflation lag and whether principal has a floor at maturity.","These bonds hedge the referenced index imperfectly: personal spending may differ, and a market price can fall when real yields rise. The gap between comparable nominal and real yields is an inflation breakeven, influenced by risk and liquidity as well as expected inflation.","Check the index, lag, floor, tax treatment of indexation and sensitivity to real interest rates."],
["Zero-coupon bonds and strips","A conventional zero pays no periodic interest and generally redeems at face value. A strip is typically a single coupon or principal payment separated from an existing bond and sold as its own security.","A zero concentrates all promised cash at maturity, which avoids reinvesting coupons but increases rate sensitivity relative to a coupon bond of the same maturity. Some jurisdictions tax accrued income before cash is received.","Check expected redemption, date, issuer or underlying security, tax timing and how much the market value could fall if yields rise."],
["Floating-rate notes (FRNs)","Coupon resets periodically to a reference rate plus a stated spread. Some notes include floors or caps, which change how closely income follows the benchmark.","Resetting coupons generally reduces benchmark-rate sensitivity compared with similar fixed-rate debt, but spreads can widen and liquidity can dry up. Income can fall when reference rates decline, and a reset lag can matter in fast markets.","Read the benchmark, spread, reset frequency, cap or floor, fallback language and any call terms."]
],
returns:[
"Bond return comes from coupons, changes in market price, and reinvestment of cash received; foreign-currency returns also depend on exchange rates. Current yield is coupon divided by price. Yield to maturity is the discount rate that equates promised cash flows to the purchase price. Realising it as a compound return assumes timely payments, holding to maturity and reinvesting coupons at that yield.",
"Modified duration gives an approximate percentage price change for a small yield move, with the opposite sign. Duration of 5 implies about a 5% fall for a one-percentage-point yield rise, before convexity. Callable bonds may offer less upside when rates fall; compare yield to the contractual call dates as well as maturity."
],
terms:[["Par, clean and dirty price","Par is face value. Clean price excludes accrued coupon; dirty price includes it and is normally closer to the settlement amount."],["Coupon, current yield and YTM","The coupon sets contractual interest. Current yield ignores capital gains or losses; YTM incorporates the scheduled cash flows and price."],["Yield to worst","Lowest of the relevant stated yields to maturity and contractual early redemption dates under the calculation convention; not a worst-case return in default."],["Duration and convexity","Duration estimates first-order price sensitivity; convexity captures curvature as yields move."],["Credit spread and recovery","Yield above a comparison benchmark; recovery is the amount received after default as a share of the claim."],["Covenant, seniority and call","Contractual protection, rank among claims, and the issuer’s option to redeem early."]],
checks:["Read the offering document and identify exact cash flows, seniority, currency and options. Compare yield to worst and duration on a like-for-like basis.","Stress-test a higher benchmark yield, wider spread and a case where the issuer cannot refinance. Assess early sale costs if the holding might need to be liquidated.","Distinguish a single bond from an ordinary bond fund, which normally rolls holdings and has no date at which you personally receive par."],
mistakes:["Assuming high coupon means high return, or assuming a bond’s mark-to-market loss disappears if the issuer defaults.","Comparing tax-exempt, callable and fixed-maturity yields without adjusting for their differences.","Treating credit ratings as insurance or forgetting that duration and maturity are different."],
reading:[["FINRA: Bonds","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/Bonds"],["FINRA: Interest-rate changes and duration","https://www.finra.org/investors/insights/bonds-interest-rate-changes-duration"]]
},
hyb:{
intro:[
"A hybrid bundles debt-like and equity-like claims in one instrument. That can mean a scheduled payment with loss-absorbing terms, an option to acquire shares, or subordination that brings the investor closer to shareholders in the capital structure. The label does not tell you which rights apply.",
"Start with the legal terms, not the coupon. Work out who can cancel or defer payments, who can convert or call the instrument, whether it ever has to be redeemed, and who absorbs losses first. Different issues from the same issuer can behave very differently."
],
types:[
["Preferred shares","Preferred stock typically ranks ahead of common shares for distributions and liquidation, but behind debt. It often has limited votes and can have a fixed, floating or reset dividend. Cumulative terms generally carry unpaid dividends forward; non-cumulative terms do not.","Payment is not the same as a bond coupon obligation. Perpetual preferreds can be highly rate-sensitive, and callable preferreds can be redeemed when refinancing is attractive to the issuer. In distress even preferred shareholders may recover nothing.","Check cumulative status, dividend restrictions, call and reset dates, liquidation preference, ranking and tax treatment."],
["Convertible bonds","A conventional convertible is a bond plus a holder’s right, under specified terms, to convert into shares. The conversion ratio determines the number of shares. Terms may allow issuer calls, investor puts, cash settlement or adjustments after certain corporate actions.","When the stock is far below the conversion price, credit and rates tend to dominate. When far above, the instrument becomes more equity-sensitive. A theoretical bond floor is an estimate, not protection against issuer distress. Mandatory and reverse convertibles allocate equity downside differently from a conventional holder-option bond.","Compare market price with conversion value and estimated straight-bond value. Verify settlement method, anti-dilution, forced conversion and issuer call terms."],
["Contingent convertibles (CoCos and bank AT1)","CoCos absorb losses when a specified trigger is met. Depending on contract and jurisdiction, that could be a regulatory capital threshold or a regulator’s determination of non-viability. Loss absorption may be by conversion into shares or a temporary or permanent write-down. Many bank AT1 securities are perpetual and have discretionary coupons.","These are not ordinary investor-option convertible bonds. Coupon cancellation can occur without a normal debt default, and a first call date is not a maturity. How investors recover in resolution depends on the legal regime and instrument terms; do not infer it from marketing labels.","Read the trigger language, capital definition, write-down or share conversion mechanics, distribution restrictions, regulator permissions and resolution hierarchy."],
["Subordinated and perpetual bonds","Subordination describes rank, while perpetuity describes maturity; a security can have either or both. A dated subordinated bond has a maturity but ranks below specified senior claims. A perpetual can have optional calls and coupon resets but no scheduled final repayment.","If an expected call is skipped, the holding period can extend and price can fall. A reset rate may make extension more or less painful, depending on benchmark and spread. Recovery depends on the debtor entity and higher-ranking claims, not only the issuer brand.","Compare yield to call with a no-call scenario. Read the deferral terms, reset spread, whether unpaid coupons accumulate, and actual ranking at the issuing entity."]
],
returns:[
"Income may come from coupons or dividends; gains or losses can come from rate moves, credit spreads, equity movements, conversion, redemption or write-down. A higher headline yield often pays for a right that belongs to the issuer or for a loss the holder agrees to bear.",
"Suppose a €1,000 convertible allows conversion into 20 shares. The conversion price is €50. At a €45 stock price, the shares are worth €900. A quoted convertible price of €1,080 is 20% above that conversion value. Whether that premium is justified depends on the value of the straight bond, remaining option life, stock volatility and credit quality."
],
terms:[["Conversion ratio and price","Shares per bond, and face amount divided by that ratio for a simple conversion."],["Conversion value and premium","Shares receivable times share price; the premium compares the instrument price with conversion value."],["Bond floor","Estimated value of the straight debt component without the conversion option. It is model-dependent and can fall."],["Call, reset and extension risk","Issuer redemption option, coupon repricing rule, and the risk that an expected redemption does not occur."],["Loss absorption","Contractual or regulatory conversion or write-down that reduces the original claim."]],
checks:["Draw the issuer’s capital stack and identify the issuing entity, senior claims and applicable resolution regime.","Model the instrument if rates rise, spreads widen, the stock falls, a coupon is missed and the first call is skipped. Add a trigger scenario if relevant.","Compare all contractual outcomes with simpler debt and equities from the same issuer. Read the prospectus for limits on payment, call and conversion."],
mistakes:["Treating the first call date as a promise, or comparing yield to a senior bond with different loss rights.","Assuming all preferred dividends accumulate or that all convertibles give the holder the same choice.","Equating a stated capital trigger with the only path to loss."],
reading:[["BIS: CoCos, a primer","https://www.bis.org/publications/cocos-primer_1.pdf"],["FINRA: Fixed-income glossary, including conversion terms","https://www.finra.org/finra-data/fixed-income/cmo/glossary"]]
},
der:{
intro:[
"A derivative is a contract whose value depends on a reference such as a share, currency, commodity, interest rate, index or credit event. It can transfer a risk, create exposure without buying the underlying, or express a view. An effective hedge offsets an existing exposure; the same contract traded by itself can add substantial risk.",
"Distinguish notional exposure from cash committed. A small premium or initial margin may control a much larger position. Leverage magnifies gains and losses relative to cash posted, and a profitable hedge at final settlement can still create a cash shortfall if collateral is due sooner."
],
types:[
["Options: calls and puts","A call gives the buyer a right to buy or receive the corresponding cash payoff; a put gives a right to sell or receive its cash equivalent. Strike, expiry, exercise style and contract multiplier define the right. American-style options can generally be exercised before expiry; European-style options only at expiry.","A buyer normally risks the premium, excluding what happens in a new position created by exercise. A seller receives the premium but can face substantial losses; an uncovered short call has theoretically unlimited loss. Before expiry, price reflects time and implied volatility as well as intrinsic value.","Read exercise and assignment rules, settlement, multipliers, spreads and collateral. Check whether hedge expiry matches the risk period."],
["Futures","Futures are standardised exchange contracts. A clearinghouse intermediates cleared positions, and gains and losses are generally settled through daily variation margin. Contracts may be physically delivered or cash-settled.","Initial margin is a performance deposit, not a loss cap. Brokers can demand more collateral or liquidate positions. A hedge can track imperfectly if location, grade, currency, delivery month or underlying differ; the difference is basis risk.","Check tick value, multiplier, expiry, last trading and delivery dates, margin requirements and how rolling changes exposure."],
["Forwards","Forwards are commonly bilateral, over-the-counter contracts that fix a price for a future purchase, sale or cash settlement. Terms can be tailored to amount, underlying and date. A non-deliverable FX forward exchanges a net cash amount instead of both currencies.","A tailored hedge can match a cash flow better than a standard future, but closing early may be expensive or require consent. Collateral, netting and counterparty credit are contract-dependent; a fully effective hedge on paper can fail if the other party cannot pay.","Match the underlying exposure, settlement currency and date. Review collateral, break costs, early termination, netting and credit quality."],
["Swaps","Swaps exchange sets of cash flows under agreed formulas. A plain interest-rate swap might exchange fixed for floating payments on a notional amount without exchanging principal. Currency swaps can exchange principal; total-return swaps exchange an asset’s return for a financing leg; credit default swaps exchange a premium for contingent protection.","A swap can transform risk rather than remove it. A borrower who pays fixed and receives floating may offset floating-rate debt, but mismatched reference rates and reset dates leave basis risk. Counterparty exposures, collateral calls and break costs can be material.","Check notional, both cash-flow schedules, benchmark fallbacks, credit-event definitions if applicable, collateral and close-out provisions."],
["Warrants and other option-like products","Warrants give rights tied to a company’s shares or another reference. Company-issued equity warrants can create new shares on exercise, potentially diluting existing owners. Bank-issued covered warrants usually give exposure under the issuer’s product terms and do not imply new shares in the underlying company.","Long tenor does not guarantee liquidity. A warrant’s price can be sensitive to fees, implied volatility and issuer credit. Structured products may embed options that cap gains or condition losses on barriers, in addition to the credit risk of the issuer.","Check the warrant ratio, strike, expiry, exercise route, issuer obligations, barrier or cap terms and secondary-market spread."]
],
returns:[
"Futures and simple forwards have a broadly linear payoff: a long position benefits when the relevant settlement price exceeds the contracted price. Options are nonlinear. A call struck at €100 bought for €4 returns €6 per unit at a €110 expiry price, or loses €4 at or below €100, before costs. A multiplier of 100 makes those amounts +€600 and −€400.",
"Before expiry, delta measures sensitivity to the underlying, gamma explains how delta changes, vega measures volatility sensitivity, and theta approximates time sensitivity. These measures are local estimates, not guarantees. For a hedge, judge the combined position after costs, not whether the derivative made money on its own."
],
terms:[["Underlying, notional and multiplier","The reference asset, contractual scale and factor converting a quoted move to cash profit or loss."],["Strike, premium and intrinsic value","Exercise price, option price, and positive exercise value ignoring any remaining time value."],["Initial and variation margin","Collateral posted to open a position and payments or collateral transfers reflecting mark-to-market moves."],["Basis and roll","The difference between related prices and the replacement of an expiring position with a later contract."],["Physical versus cash settlement","Transfer of the underlying or settlement of the defined cash amount."],["Implied volatility and Greeks","Volatility inferred from option prices and measures of sensitivity to key inputs."]],
checks:["Write the payoff under several scenarios, including overnight gaps and a move of the underlying without the expected move in the hedge.","Translate per-unit moves into currency profit or loss, and estimate peak margin needs. Check broker liquidation and exercise rules.","Read contract specifications and document the purpose, maximum tolerable loss, unwind plan, counterparty and settlement workflow."],
mistakes:["Confusing margin or premium with notional, or assuming stop orders cap losses when markets gap.","Thinking a profitable expiry payoff describes the entire path or that a clearinghouse removes all risks.","Overlooking assignment, physical delivery, expiry conventions, bid–ask spreads and roll costs."],
reading:[["CME Group: Futures versus forwards","https://www.cmegroup.com/articles/2025/futurization-futures-vs-forwards.html"],["CME Group: Options on futures","https://www.cmegroup.com/content/dam/cmegroup/education/files/options-on-futures-brochure.pdf"],["BIS glossary: Interest-rate swaps","https://data.bis.org/help/glossary?item=interest%20rate%20swap"]]
},
fund:{
intro:[
"A pooled fund combines investors’ money in a portfolio. It is a vehicle, not a single underlying asset class: a bond fund can carry duration and credit risk, an equity fund can carry stock-market risk, and a commodity product may depend on futures. Identify the holdings and exposures separately from the structure that gives you access.",
"A fund can be open-end, exchange-traded or closed-end; it can also be indexed or actively managed. Those labels describe different things. For example, both mutual funds and ETFs can follow an index or use an active manager."
],
types:[
["Open-end mutual funds","A conventional open-end mutual fund issues and redeems units directly or through intermediaries, usually at the next calculated net asset value (NAV) after a cut-off. Some jurisdictions or objectives use different dealing schedules. Share classes can differ in fees, minimums, currency and distribution policy.","Daily dealing does not mean the underlying assets are all liquid. Funds may use swing pricing or other liquidity tools; permissible restrictions or suspensions depend on local law and fund terms. Sales loads and redemption charges can materially change net returns.","Compare share-class expenses, dealing frequency, valuation cut-off, redemption rules, and tax treatment."],
["Exchange-traded funds (ETFs)","ETF shares trade intraday. Authorised participants can generally create or redeem large blocks with the fund, using securities or cash. That mechanism tends to keep market price near portfolio value but does not guarantee it.","Investors pay the bid–ask spread and may transact above or below NAV, especially in stress or when underlying markets are closed. A synthetic ETF may use swaps and collateral. Leveraged and inverse ETFs often target daily results, so multiday performance depends on the path and compounding.","Identify the structure, underlying-market hours, replication method, spread, tracking history and any daily reset. An exchange-traded note is typically issuer debt, not an ETF, even if it looks similar on a screen."],
["Closed-end funds","A traditional listed closed-end fund generally raises capital at launch and its shares then trade between investors rather than being routinely redeemed at NAV. Some funds issue new shares, buy back shares or conduct tender offers. Other closed-end structures, such as interval funds, may offer only periodic limited repurchases.","A discount may persist or widen; it is not a guaranteed gain. Leverage can amplify losses, and a distribution may include a return of capital rather than just earned income. Non-listed vehicles can be especially difficult to exit.","Compare NAV and market-price performance, distribution sources, financing costs, leverage limits and any credible mechanism for closing a discount."],
["Index funds","An index fund aims to replicate a benchmark using full replication, a sample of holdings or, where allowed, derivatives. The index rulebook dictates inclusion, weights and rebalancing. Some indexes concentrate heavily in their largest holdings or target narrow themes.","Tracking difference is the return gap versus the benchmark over a period. Tracking error is the variability of that gap. Fund fees, taxes, sampling, trading costs, securities lending and the comparison index version all affect results.","Read the methodology and check concentration, turnover, replication, and whether performance is compared with the correct price or total-return benchmark."],
["Actively managed funds","A manager selects securities or changes exposures according to a mandate, aiming for a goal such as beating a benchmark, generating income or managing volatility. Judging performance requires knowing both the objective and the risks the manager is allowed to take.","Outperformance may come from factor exposure or taking more risk, not repeatable manager skill. Results can change with staff turnover, fund size, style drift or market regime. Higher costs raise the hurdle for net outperformance.","Compare net results against an appropriate benchmark across cycles, review attribution and drawdowns, and investigate manager changes, fees and turnover."]
],
returns:[
"A fund passes through the portfolio’s economic returns after expenses, through NAV change and distributions. Accumulating share classes reinvest income internally; distributing classes pay it out. The cash payout is not an extra return on top of the NAV before distribution, and reinvestment does not necessarily remove tax obligations.",
"Compare total costs over your expected holding period: ongoing charges, platform and trading costs, spreads, portfolio turnover and any performance fee. For example, €10,000 growing at 6% gross annually for 20 years becomes about €32,071. At an illustrative 5% net, it becomes about €26,533 before tax."
],
terms:[["NAV per unit","Assets minus liabilities, divided by units outstanding."],["Ongoing charges and total cost","Recurring fund costs; separately consider brokerage, spreads, transaction costs, platform fees and taxes."],["Tracking difference and tracking error","The level of the return gap and how variable that gap is, respectively."],["Premium and discount","Market price above or below NAV; changes affect an investor’s realised market-price return."],["Share class and currency hedge","A class may have distinct fee and distribution terms; hedged classes try to reduce specified currency exposure, not all investment risk."],["Domicile and index methodology","The fund’s legal home and the benchmark’s rulebook."]],
checks:["Read the prospectus or applicable key information document, factsheet and holdings. Compare top weights, derivatives, leverage, duration, credit and currency as applicable.","Look through multiple funds for duplicate exposures. A broad-sounding name or a large holdings count does not prove diversification.","Compare costs, net performance, redemption terms, domicile, replication and securities-lending policies. Check which currency is quoted and which exposures are hedged."],
mistakes:["Treating every ETF as passive, every fund as diversified, or all listed products as regulated funds of the same kind.","Chasing prior-year performance or comparing a fund’s gross return with a benchmark on a different basis.","Ignoring spread or fund discounts because ongoing fees appear low."],
reading:[["SEC: Guide to mutual funds and ETFs","https://www.investor.gov/sites/investorgov/files/mutual-funds-ETFs.pdf"],["Investor.gov: Investment glossary, including closed-end funds","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/glossary/all"]]
},
abs:{
intro:[
"Securitisation turns claims on many assets into securities. In a typical funded transaction an originator sells loans or receivables to a special-purpose vehicle (SPV), and the SPV issues securities backed by collections. A servicer collects payments, manages arrears and remits cash. In a synthetic transaction, selected credit risk can be transferred without selling the loans.",
"Analyse both the pool and the contract. The same loan pool can support a number of securities with very different loss exposure because the waterfall specifies where payments go and which claims absorb losses. Covered bonds are studied alongside these instruments because they are asset-backed funding, although their dual-recourse structure is distinct from traditional securitisation."
],
types:[
["Mortgage-backed securities (RMBS and CMBS)","RMBS use residential mortgages; CMBS use commercial-property loans. A pass-through passes principal and interest to investors after fees. A multiclass structure can redirect principal across tranches. Some agency mortgage securities have specified guarantees; non-agency credit protection depends on the deal.","Residential refinancing can return principal early when rates fall and extend average life when rates rise. CMBS can be exposed to tenant departures, falling appraisals, concentrated properties and balloon refinancing risk. A guarantee of specified payments does not fix market price or reinvestment risk.","Check guarantee terms, loan-to-value, debt-service coverage, occupancy as relevant, geography, loan concentration and restrictions on prepayment."],
["Consumer and equipment asset-backed securities (ABS)","Auto ABS can be backed by amortising loans or leases; credit-card ABS often use revolving receivables and may reinvest principal during a revolving period. Student-loan collateral may have private or public support depending on the loans. Equipment receivables and leases can depend on asset resale values.","The risks differ by collateral: used-car prices affect auto recoveries; unemployment and payment rates affect card losses; equipment leases may have residual-value risk. A trigger can end a revolving period early, accelerating return of principal.","Inspect vintage-level delinquencies, underwriting, charge-offs, recoveries, servicing standards and whether collateral can be substituted."],
["CLOs and related debt structures","A CLO usually pools corporate loans, often below investment grade. A manager may trade them during a reinvestment period. A CLO issues several debt tranches plus a residual equity interest. Other collateralised debt structures can hold different assets; the names do not imply the same risk.","Coverage-test failures can divert cash from junior securities toward paying senior debt. Junior investors are therefore sensitive to defaults, recoveries, spread changes and manager choices. Even a senior tranche can show large market losses or trade poorly during stress.","Review the eligibility rules, loan concentrations, overcollateralisation and interest-coverage cushions, reinvestment end date, manager record and refinancing rights."],
["Covered bonds","A covered bond is generally a bank obligation with an additional preferential claim on a designated cover pool under the applicable framework. The investor normally has dual recourse: to the issuer and, if needed, to eligible cover assets. The cover pool is generally retained on the issuer’s balance sheet and segregated under the local framework.","Protection depends on the governing regime and issue terms. Cover assets may be replaced over time. A soft-bullet bond can extend maturity in specified circumstances; dual recourse does not make the bond risk-free.","Check issuer credit, cover-pool composition and eligibility, required overcollateralisation, asset-liability mismatch and extension triggers."]
],
returns:[
"Cash can come from borrower interest, scheduled amortisation, voluntary prepayments and recoveries, less fees and losses. Principal arriving sooner can be positive or negative depending on the price paid and available reinvestment yields. Find out whether a tranche receives principal sequentially, pro rata or under a changing set of triggers.",
"Expected return depends on scenarios rather than on a single maturity date. Compare weighted-average life, spreads and cash flows under faster and slower prepayment and different default and recovery paths. High quoted spread may reflect optionality, weak collateral, model uncertainty or difficulty selling, not just credit risk."
],
terms:[["SPV, originator and servicer","The issuing vehicle, the party that created the assets, and the party that administers collections."],["Waterfall and tranche","Payment priority and a defined slice of risk and return."],["Attachment and detachment points","Pool loss levels at which a tranche starts and finishes absorbing losses in a simplified capital structure."],["Credit enhancement","Subordination, reserves, excess spread, guarantees or collateral above liabilities. Check each protection’s limits."],["Weighted-average life","Average time to principal repayments, weighted by amounts, which changes when cash flows change."],["Prepayment and extension","Principal paid ahead of schedule and the risk that expected principal instead comes later."],["Coverage tests","Contractual tests that can redirect cash or change the waterfall."]],
checks:["Start with offering documents, loan-level or pool reports, trustee data and servicer history. Compare borrower quality, collateral, geography, vintage and concentrations.","Draw the waterfall and model how correlated defaults, lower recoveries, earlier or later payments and a trigger breach change your tranche’s cash flows.","Read which assumptions are in pricing models and ratings. Compare quoted spreads with similar structures and check actual trading liquidity."],
mistakes:["Treating a senior rating as protection against market losses, illiquidity or a faulty cash-flow model.","Treating all mortgage bonds alike, or confusing covered bonds’ dual recourse with a traditional SPV security.","Assuming stable historic defaults continue when many loans share the same economic risk."],
reading:[["ECB: Securitisation in the euro area","https://www.ecb.europa.eu/pub/pdf/other/pp81-94_mb200802en.pdf"],["Federal Reserve: Collateralized loan obligations","https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/notes/feds-notes/collateralized-loan-obligations-in-the-financial-accounts-of-the-united-states-20190920.html"],["European Banking Authority: Covered bond frameworks","https://www.eba.europa.eu/sites/default/files/documents/10180/1699643/d2b08b75-601f-4de3-ba36-ad674cdc0d46/EBA%20Report%20on%20Covered%20Bonds%20(EBA-Op-2016-23).pdf"]]
},
cash:{
intro:[
"Cash is money available to spend or transfer. Cash equivalents and money market instruments are designed for short horizons and cash management, but can differ sharply in legal protection and access. A savings account is an obligation of a bank, a Treasury bill is government debt, and a money market fund share is a claim on a portfolio.",
"Judge this category by three questions: When exactly can I access the money? Who owes it to me and what protection applies? What will I earn after costs, tax, inflation and currency conversion? A modest increase in yield does not always compensate for losing immediate access or taking additional credit risk."
],
types:[
["Treasury bills","Bills are short-term sovereign obligations. They commonly pay no periodic coupon; a buyer purchasing below par earns the difference at redemption, assuming payment. Bills can be bought at auction or traded in a secondary market, depending on market access.","Short maturities limit rate sensitivity but do not eliminate price moves if sold early. Credit risk varies by sovereign, and a bill in a foreign currency can lose more from exchange-rate movements than it earns in interest.","Check the issuing government, denomination, maturity, settlement, purchase method and annualisation convention."],
["Certificates of deposit (CDs) and term deposits","A fixed-term deposit locks funds with a bank for an agreed period. Some CDs can be traded, while non-negotiable retail deposits may be inaccessible before maturity or subject to a penalty. Callable CDs can return your principal early under the terms.","A quoted rate can be attractive until an early withdrawal penalty, reinvestment at lower rates, or a brokered CD’s secondary-market loss is included. Eligible bank deposits may be protected, but rules depend on scheme, institution and account ownership.","Verify which institution holds the deposit, eligibility and coverage, effective annual rate, maturity, rollover, early exit and call terms."],
["Commercial paper","Commercial paper is short-term borrowing by businesses or financial institutions, often issued at a discount. Traditional commercial paper is generally unsecured; asset-backed commercial paper relies on a conduit’s assets and contractual support.","A frequent need to roll over paper exposes issuers to funding stress. Short maturity does not prevent losses if confidence deteriorates, and credit or liquidity support is only as good as its terms and providers.","Assess issuer and guarantor credit, maturity, available back-up liquidity, assets for a conduit, and investor access to the secondary market."],
["Repurchase agreements (repos)","A repo is economically a collateralised loan: one party sells securities and agrees to repurchase them at a higher price on a specified date. The cash lender calls its side a reverse repo. Overnight and term repos differ in maturity; bilateral and tri-party arrangements differ in administration.","Collateral mitigates but does not eliminate loss. A haircut provides a buffer if collateral value falls. A default can leave the lender exposed to price gaps, legal enforceability, collateral liquidity and settlement difficulties.","Review counterparty, collateral eligibility, haircut, mark-to-market and margin process, repurchase date and close-out rights."],
["Money market funds","A money market fund pools short-dated instruments. Depending on its mandate it may focus on government debt and repos, or also hold private short-term debt. Some funds target a stable NAV under applicable rules, while others use a floating NAV.","A fund is not a bank deposit. A stable target is not a guarantee. Liquidity fees, timing restrictions or other stress measures can apply depending on rules and documents. A distribution yield can change quickly as holdings mature and rates reset.","Read reporting on maturity, liquidity and issuer mix; compare net yield on the same basis; confirm NAV convention, redemption cut-off and settlement."],
["Savings and deposit accounts","A savings account typically pays variable interest on balances; notice accounts may require advance notice for withdrawals. Current or transaction accounts focus on payments, and some pay interest. Savings offers can apply temporary rates, balance tiers or minimum conditions.","Deposit protection, where eligible, generally depends on the legally licensed institution and applicable scheme, not on the app or brand name. Balances at brands sharing a licence may count together. A brokerage sweep or payment-app balance is not automatically a covered bank deposit.","Check the account provider and scheme directly, including aggregation, ownership category, promotional expiry, fees, withdrawal notice and currency."]
],
returns:[
"Money market yields can quote different denominators and day counts. A bill’s simple holding-period return is (face value minus price) divided by price. A bank-discount quote instead uses face value and, in US practice, a 360-day basis. An effective annual deposit rate includes compounding; check the conventions before comparing it with a simple annualised bill return.",
"Inflation can erode purchasing power even if nominal capital is intact. With an illustrative effective nominal return of 3% and inflation of 4%, the real return is 1.03 ÷ 1.04 − 1, or about −0.96% before tax. A ladder of staggered maturities can spread reinvestment dates, but not eliminate the risk of lower future rates."
],
terms:[["Maturity versus settlement","Contractual payment date versus when cash actually changes hands after trading or redemption."],["Discount yield and investment yield","Quoted conventions using different denominators and sometimes different day counts."],["Effective annual yield","Annual rate including compounding under the stated assumptions."],["Deposit protection","A legal scheme for eligible deposits, subject to local rules, limits and exclusions."],["Haircut","Buffer applied to collateral value when determining how much cash can be lent."],["Rollover and liquidity risk","Risk of refinancing on worse terms or being unable to get cash when needed."]],
checks:["Map each cash need to an exact date and currency; keep near-term needs separate from instruments with notice, sale or maturity risk.","Identify the legal bank, issuer or fund; check deposit eligibility separately from investment risk. Compare yields net of fees, tax and penalties on a common basis.","Plan the path back to spendable cash: market hours, cut-off times, settlement, transfer limits and what happens at automatic renewal."],
mistakes:["Calling a fund or broker balance insured without identifying the product and scheme.","Paying a penalty to break a deposit because a headline yield distracted from the maturity.","Using foreign-currency yield as a like-for-like comparison with home-currency cash."],
reading:[["TreasuryDirect: US Treasury bills","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/treasury-bills/"],["Federal Reserve Bank of New York: Repo and reverse repo","https://www.newyorkfed.org/markets/domestic-market-operations/monetary-policy-implementation/repo-reverse-repo-agreements"],["Federal Reserve: Commercial paper background","https://www.federalreserve.gov/regreform/reform-cpff.htm"],["SEC investor bulletin: Money market funds","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/updated-12"],["De Nederlandsche Bank: Dutch deposit guarantee","https://www.dnb.nl/en/reliable-financial-sector/dutch-deposit-guarantee/faq-dutch-deposit-guarantee/"]]
},
alt:{
intro:[
"Alternatives group together exposures outside the conventional stock, bond and cash categories. The label can describe the underlying asset, an investment strategy or a less liquid vehicle. A listed property trust and a private property partnership may share real estate exposure while having very different trading, leverage, valuation and redemption terms.",
"Start with the economic source of return and the route to exit. Private appraisal marks can move less often than exchange prices without the assets being safer. Low reported correlation may reflect stale marks or may increase during stress as financing tightens."
],
types:[
["Direct real estate and REITs","Direct property earns rent and can appreciate, with costs for vacancy, repairs, capital expenditure, financing and transactions. Listed equity REITs own income-producing properties; mortgage REITs often hold mortgages or mortgage securities and have different financing risks. Non-listed real estate vehicles may have limited redemptions.","Property value depends on rent, occupancy, local supply and required capitalisation rates. Debt amplifies gains and losses; refinancing can force a sale in a weak market. Listed REITs can trade very differently from appraised property values in the short run.","Compare net operating income, capital expenditure, tenant concentration, loan-to-value, lease and debt maturities. For equity REITs, review funds from operations (FFO) and reconcile any adjusted measure to reported figures."],
["Private equity and venture capital","Buyout funds acquire operating businesses and may use significant borrowing. Growth equity typically funds expansion of established businesses. Venture capital backs younger companies, often in successive rounds; a few winners can account for much of a fund’s return.","Capital calls and distributions arrive on uncertain dates, and early net returns can be negative before exits (the J-curve). Operating performance, dilution, leverage and exit valuations all matter. Private valuations are estimates; secondaries may trade below reported NAV.","Check strategy, vintage, leverage, fund term, uncalled commitments, fee and carry waterfall, and realised proceeds. Compare IRR with TVPI and DPI; a strong mark is not cash distributed."],
["Hedge funds","Hedge funds are vehicles with strategies such as long/short equity, global macro, event-driven, managed futures or relative value. Some seek returns less dependent on broad markets; others deliberately take directional risk. Short selling, borrowing and derivatives can change exposure considerably.","A low net exposure can conceal high gross exposure, leverage and crowded positions. Short positions can incur large losses and borrowing costs. Lock-ups, gates and side pockets may constrain exits when markets are stressed.","Understand strategy, gross and net exposure, collateral, financing and redemption. Check administrator, auditor, manager incentives, high-water mark, and tail losses in stressed periods."],
["Commodities","Energy, metals and agricultural goods respond to supply, demand, inventories, weather, transport and policy. Exposure can come from physical ownership, futures-linked funds or commodity producer shares. Producer shares add company and financing risks and need not track the commodity.","Physical holdings may require storage and insurance. Futures exposure depends on the term structure: in contango, later contracts are more expensive than nearer ones; a long strategy that rolls can face negative roll effects, all else equal. Backwardation can have the opposite effect; overall returns also depend on price changes and collateral income.","Check what is held, benchmark and roll schedule, collateral income, fees, storage and whether the product is a fund or issuer note."],
["Infrastructure","Infrastructure includes assets such as toll roads, utilities, renewable power and digital networks. Mature brownfield projects already operate; greenfield developments carry construction and commissioning risk. Cash flows may rely on regulated tariffs, availability payments, long-term contracts or merchant prices.","Inflation protection depends on actual indexation clauses and reset lags. Planning delays, cost overruns, regulation, counterparty failure or refinancing can dominate returns even when asset demand looks stable.","Read the concession or contract, offtaker credit, traffic or price assumptions, construction guarantees, maintenance obligations and debt-service coverage."],
["Private credit","Private credit includes directly originated corporate loans, asset-based finance, mezzanine debt and distressed lending. Loans can be secured, floating-rate or covenant-heavy, but terms and protections vary. Coupon income may include payment-in-kind interest, which increases principal rather than providing cash now.","Floating rates can raise lender income and borrower stress at the same time. Amendments or extensions can defer recognition of weakness, and private marks, leverage and redemption terms can hide liquidity mismatches. Recovery depends on collateral and seniority.","Review loan-level concentration, collateral, covenant headroom, non-accruals, cash versus non-cash income, fund leverage and realistic workout recoveries."],
["Collectibles","Art, wine, coins and other scarce objects are valued through authenticity, condition, provenance, scarcity and buyer demand. Most produce no regular cash flow; value often depends on resale to a willing buyer.","Dealer and auction costs, storage, insurance and restoration can absorb gains. Quoted estimates may reflect selected transactions, and fraud or forgery can make a holding hard to insure or sell.","Verify provenance and title, independent appraisal, condition, comparables net of commissions and the practical path to sale."],
["Crypto-assets","Tokens can represent payment, access, governance or other functions, depending on their design. A token does not necessarily confer equity, cash-flow rights or a legal claim on reserve assets. Stablecoins attempt to track a reference value, but reserve quality and redemption vary.","Volatility, fraud, loss of keys, smart-contract failures, exchange insolvency and depegging can cause loss. Using a fund or exchange-traded product changes operational exposure but adds its own structure, fees and custody dependencies. Legal protections vary by product and jurisdiction.","Verify custody and segregation, who can change the protocol, liquidity, issuance and unlock schedules, redemption rights, and which protections actually apply."]
],
returns:[
"Returns may come from rent, interest, operating improvements, price appreciation, a risk premium or active decisions. Under private fund structures, separate asset-level gross return from fund-level net return after management fees, leverage, expenses, carried interest and taxes. Reported IRR is sensitive to when money goes in and out and can change if a credit line delays capital calls.",
"A comparable appraisal is not a realisable sale price. Model the proceeds net of exit costs, delay and debt; then ask how much cash is available in a downturn to meet capital calls and other commitments. Diversification is useful only if the actual underlying drivers differ from the risks already in the portfolio."
],
terms:[["Lock-up, gate and side pocket","A period without redemptions, a limit on withdrawals, and separate accounting for hard-to-sell holdings."],["Capital call and uncalled commitment","A demand for part of committed capital, and the remaining sum an investor may still have to fund."],["IRR, TVPI and DPI","Cash-flow-sensitive annualised return; total value (distributions plus remaining NAV) divided by paid-in capital; and cash distributions divided by paid-in capital."],["Carried interest and hurdle","The manager’s contractual profit share and a threshold that may have to be met first."],["Cap rate and loan-to-value","Property net operating income divided by property value; loan balance divided by collateral value."],["Appraisal smoothing","Infrequent or model-based marks can mute reported volatility while economic risk remains."],["Gross and net exposure","Gross adds long and absolute short exposure; net offsets short exposures against longs under the stated measurement convention."]],
checks:["Identify the vehicle, assets, manager, auditor and custodian. Check legal terms, investor eligibility, valuation independence and conflicts of interest.","Reconcile performance to cash distributions and expenses. Compare like strategies and vintages, assess financing and concentration, and include difficult exits and liquidity calls in stress scenarios.","Read commitment and redemption rules, fee terms and regulatory disclosures. Compare a cheaper or more transparent route to the same economic exposure."],
mistakes:["Treating smooth marks, a high internal rate of return or manager-selected deals as proof of low risk or repeatable results.","Assuming an exit right is usable at the quoted valuation or that an attractive income figure is cash received.","Grouping all alternatives together and overlooking duplicate equity, credit, rate, currency or leverage exposures."],
reading:[["SEC: Starting a private fund and common strategies","https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/starting-private-fund"],["SEC investor bulletin: Publicly traded REITs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-65"],["SEC investor bulletin: Hedge funds","https://www.sec.gov/files/investor/alerts/ib_hedgefunds.pdf"],["CME Group: How backwardation affects roll returns","https://www.cmegroup.com/insights/economic-research/2026/how-backwardation-generates-positive-roll-returns-in-energy.html"],["ESMA and other European regulators: Crypto-asset risk warning","https://www.esma.europa.eu/press-news/esma-news/eu-financial-regulators-warn-consumers-risks-crypto-assets"]]
}
};
